{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3220","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3220","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3220\",\"parent_revision\":\"38645bdb1a381aabe36ee97e14a676ee853fa76c1f42a6830f3a64265ff54fde\",\"root_revision\":\"38645bdb1a381aabe36ee97e14a676ee853fa76c1f42a6830f3a64265ff54fde\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-24\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nNothing in this repository's review path checks, before merge, that a PR actually demonstrates it resolves the issue it was opened for. Reviews assess findings; no gate assesses resolution.\\n\\nThree places could carry the check and none does, verified by direct read on 2026-08-24:\\n\\n- AGENTS.md \\\"PR Review Protocol\\\" (now two lines pointing at the receiving-pr-reviews skill) requires fetching both feedback levels and addressing all inline comments. It is silent on the originating issue.\\n- .claude/rules/review-and-correction-discipline.md defines the structural-versus-content gate split, requires the content review actually run against the diff, and requires capable adjudication for judgment checks. It is silent on issues.\\n- .agents/skills/receiving-pr-reviews/SKILL.md step 2 says to \\\"validate the claim locally, assess against the change goal and repository instructions\\\". That is per-finding validation, not per-PR resolution. No step in the skill references the originating issue, and none asks whether the change closes it.\\n\\nThe nearest existing resolution proof is dh:tn-verification-gate with dh:verify-done: it re-runs the acceptance-criteria check commands against a T0 baseline and computes per-criterion status. That is genuine evidence, but it is plan-scoped and runs before the PR exists, never as a review round against what the PR actually contains. A PR can therefore merge having passed every review round without any recorded evidence that the issue's acceptance criteria are met by the merged diff.\\n\\nImpact \u2014 this is the gate that makes the Relates to: #N convention safe. Backlog item #3219 moves dh to a non-closing reference convention so that /dh:complete-implementation, not a merge trailer, closes the issue. That shifts closure onto an assessment step, which means the assessment must exist and must be able to fail. Without it, dropping the auto-closing keyword converts premature closure into indefinite non-closure.\\n\\nImpact \u2014 measured non-closure already exists. In the seven days to 2026-08-24, 115 PRs merged and 16 declared a closing reference. Issue #3162 was fixed by merged PR #3174 and stayed open until closed by hand a day later; the PR body's `Closes #3162` sat inside a backtick code span, which GitHub does not parse as a closing keyword, so nothing linked.\\n\\nRelationship to existing items \u2014 this is distinct from #3096 and should not be folded into it. #3096 asks complete-implementation to open a PR and run a monitor, assess, fix, re-verify loop; that loop's assessment unit is the review finding. This item's assessment unit is the issue's acceptance criteria against the merged diff. The two compose: the loop is where the check would run, the check is what the loop currently lacks.\\n\\nUser-provided context: the repo owner asked whether advice exists requiring that \\\"prior to merging, the PR is checked as part of its review rounds to verify it has demonstrated its ability to resolve the issue\\\". It does not.\\n\\nPoints needing design decisions during grooming, not pre-decided here: where the check belongs (a step in receiving-pr-reviews, a phase in complete-implementation, or a separate gate); what counts as demonstrated resolution when an issue has no structured acceptance criteria; whether the evidence is recorded on the PR, on the issue, or as a SAM artifact; and what a failing verdict does to the merge, given that this repository's PRs are merged by a human rather than by an automated gate.</description>\\n</invoke>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session audit of merged-PR issue linkage, 2026-08-24\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-24\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependency Note 2026 08 24\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe `Relates to: #N` convention in #3219 is scoped to the dh workflow path only; PRs made outside\\nthat loop, in human-supervised development, may use `Fixes #N` or `Relates to: #N` at the author's\\nchoice. That narrows the dependency stated above: deferred closure, and therefore the risk that\\ndropping the auto-closing keyword turns premature closure into indefinite non-closure, applies on\\nthe dh path. Outside it, `Fixes #N` still closes on merge and no gate is required to reach closure.\\n\\nThis does not narrow the item itself. The check the repo owner asked for \u2014 that before merge a PR\\nis verified to have demonstrated it resolves its issue \u2014 is worth having on both paths, and is\\nabsent from both today. The non-dh path simply has a weaker argument for it: closure happens\\neither way, so the gate protects correctness rather than reachability. Whether the gate is scoped\\nto dh or applies to any PR is a grooming decision, and it interacts with the last open question\\nabove, since a human merges these PRs and a failing verdict has no automated merge to block.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Settled Design 2026 08 24 Grilling Session\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nContinuing the design session recorded on #3219. The evidence-trail and guard questions belong\\nhere, since this item's assessment unit is resolution correctness, not linking-convention text.\\n\\n### The evidence trail is contractual on every backend (Q4, repo owner: \\\"its part of what the workflow should be ensuring\\\")\\n\\nVerified by direct read: `resolve_item()` (`operations.py:3180`) requires `summary` (raises\\n`ValidationError` if empty) and accepts `method`, `notes`, `follow_ups`, `findings` \u2014 but forwards\\nall five only to `resolve_github_issue()`. `BacklogItemMetadata` (`models.py`) has no field for\\nany of them; it has `close_reason` for the dismissal path but nothing for resolution. On a\\nnon-GitHub backend the required evidence trail is written nowhere \u2014 the caller is compelled to\\nsupply an audit trail that is silently discarded.\\n\\nSettled: add a `resolution`-shaped field to `BacklogItemMetadata` (summary, method, notes,\\nfollow_ups, findings), and have `resolve_item()` persist it via `update_item_metadata()` on every\\nbackend before the GitHub-specific branch runs. The GitHub `## Resolved` comment is then\\n*generated from* that persisted record \u2014 a rendering, not a second source of truth. This is the\\nsame shape as the `Fixes #N` trailer defect on #3219 (closure/evidence encoded in a\\nGitHub-specific artifact instead of the neutral item), applied to evidence instead of closure\\nstate.\\n\\n### The open-PR guard needs an explicit `--pr` to distinguish the delivering PR from others (Q5/A3=c)\\n\\n`resolve_item()` calls `check_open_prs_for_issue()` (`gh_client.py:1360`), a blind\\n`is:pr is:open #{N}` search, and raises `BacklogError` (\\\"Resolving will close the issue and orphan\\nthese PRs\\\") if anything matches. At `/dh:complete-implementation` step 4, the PR pushed at step 2\\nis *necessarily* still open and matches \u2014 the guard's own message is false in this call site, since\\nresolving does not orphan the PR that delivered the work.\\n\\nConsidered and rejected: passing `force=True` from `complete-implementation` (disables the guard\\nentirely, including for genuinely outstanding other PRs) and excluding the current branch by query\\nconstruction (works only when the caller has a \\\"current branch\\\" to infer from). Settled: add an\\nexplicit `--pr <N>` argument to the resolve call, naming the delivering PR so the guard can exclude\\nit from its result set before deciding whether to raise. Auditable (the caller states what it's\\nexcusing, not inferred from branch state) and works from any caller, not only a branch-aware one.\\n\\nScope check performed during the session: this closes the immediate `resolve_item` call site, not\\nthe underlying gap. `check_open_prs_for_issue` is GitHub-only, so **Beads has no open-work guard at\\nresolve time at all today** \u2014 a Beads item can be resolved while other work depends on it with no\\nwarning. Modeling \\\"which PR delivers this item\\\" as a real, backend-neutral relationship (rather\\nthan a GitHub-only live search with a one-shot exclusion argument) is filed separately as new\\nschema surface, not folded into this fix.\\n\\n### Where this leaves the item\\n\\nThe dependency stated in the original description holds and is now more precisely scoped: on the\\ndh path, resolution happens through a single CLI/MCP call (#3219), so this item's evidence-trail\\npersistence and guard fix are what make that call trustworthy \u2014 a resolve that silently discards\\nits audit trail or falsely blocks on the PR that just delivered the work is not a gate that\\nprotects anything. The originally-open question of where the resolution-verification check belongs\\n(dh-scoped vs any PR) is unaffected by this update and remains for grooming.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"3adbf8dbccd434e47cb61792954e7d861092ba453c2404fc1948a8caec3b9ca3\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQg4X-A\"}"}